Prompting an AI codding tool is somewhat lime communicating with a very literal, sometimes knowledgeable collaborator.
To get useful results, you need to set the stage clearly and guide the AI on what you want and how you wnat it.

Below are foundational principles that underpin all examples in this guide:

    Provide rich context 
        Always assume the AI knows nothing about your project beyond what you provide.
        Include relevant details such as programming language, frameworks, and libraries, as well as the specific cunction or snippet in question.
        If there's an error, provide the exact error message and describe what the code is supposed to do.
        Example 
            "I have a Node.js function using Express and Mongoose that should fetch a user by ID, but it throws a TypeError. Here's the code and error"

    Be specific about your goal or question 
        Vague queries led to vague answers.
        Example
            "This JavaScript function is returning undefined instead of the expected result. Given the code below, can you help identify why and how to fix it?"
        Similary, if you want an optimization, ask for a specific kind of optimization.
        Example     
            "How can I improve the runtime performance of this sorting function for 10k items?"

    Break down complex tasks 
        When implementing a new feature or takling a multi-step problem, don't feed the entire problem inone gigantic prompt.
        It's more effective to split the work into smaller chunks and iterate.
        Example
            "First, generate a React componnet skeleton for a product list page.Next, we'll add state managment. Then we'll integrate the API call"

    Include examples of Inputs/Outputs or expected behaviour 
        If you can illustrate what you want with an example
        For example 
            "Given the array [3,1,4], this function should return [1,3,4]"
        Provinding an example makes the AI understand and reduce ambiguity.
        In prompt engineering terms, this is sometimes called "few-shot prompting" Where you show an ai a pattern to follow.

    Leverage Roles or persona 
        This can influence the style and depth of the answers.
        For instance 
            " Act as a senior React developer and review my code for potential bugs"
            "You are a javascript performance expert. Optmize the following function"
        By setting up the role, you prime the assistant to  adopt the relevant tone.
        Role play prompt can yield more structured and expert-level guidance.

    Iterate and refine the conversation 
        Prompt engineering is an iterative process, not one shot deal.
        Developers need to review the AI first answer then do a follow up questions or make corrections.
        The key is to view the AI as a partner you can coach - progress over perfection.
        Example
            "That solution uses recursion, but i'd prefer an iterative approach - i can you try agnain without recursion?"

    Maintain code clarity and consistency 
        This last principle is a bit indirect but very important for tools that work on your code context.
        Write clean, well structured code and comments, even before the AI comes to play.
        If you show a consistent pattern or style, the Ai will continue it. 
        
